{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"49dd176a2a33a348","name":"card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/events/card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"COMBINED CHIEFS OF STAFF","text":"Your British and US units get +2+3.","flavorText":"The Combined Chiefs of Staff (CCS) was formed in 1942 and became the central command body for Anglo-American military planning during WWII. Operating from Washington, it brought together senior US and British military leaders to harmonize strategy across all theaters. The CCS played a decisive role in shaping Allied operations, overseeing war production priorities, logistics, and theater command appointments. One of its earliest achievements was the \"Germany First\" strategy, which prioritized defeating Nazi Germany before focusing on Japan. The CCS also approved Operation Torch, the first major Allied amphibious assault in North Africa, and later coordinated the invasions of Sicily and Italy. Perhaps its most critical contribution came with Operation Overlord. The CCS selected General Eisenhower as Supreme Commander and directed the build-up for the 1944 Normandy landings. In the Pacific, the CCS established a division of responsibility: the US led operations in the central and south-west Pacific, while British forces focused on Southeast Asia. This division allowed for coordinated offensives that overwhelmed Japanese defenses on multiple fronts. Meeting frequently throughout the war and in close contact with political leaders, the CCS ensured unity of command and purpose. Its coordinated approach was unprecedented and critical to the eventual Allied victory.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":6,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff_title","SourceString":"COMBINED CHIEFS OF STAFF","LocalizedString":"COMBINED CHIEFS OF STAFF"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":6,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff_text","SourceString":"Your British and US units get +2+3.","LocalizedString":"Your British and US units get +2+3."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff_history","SourceString":"The Combined Chiefs of Staff (CCS) was formed in 1942 and became the central command body for Anglo-American military planning during WWII. Operating from Washington, it brought together senior US and British military leaders to harmonize strategy across all theaters. The CCS played a decisive role in shaping Allied operations, overseeing war production priorities, logistics, and theater command appointments. One of its earliest achievements was the \"Germany First\" strategy, which prioritized defeating Nazi Germany before focusing on Japan. The CCS also approved Operation Torch, the first major Allied amphibious assault in North Africa, and later coordinated the invasions of Sicily and Italy. Perhaps its most critical contribution came with Operation Overlord. The CCS selected General Eisenhower as Supreme Commander and directed the build-up for the 1944 Normandy landings. In the Pacific, the CCS established a division of responsibility: the US led operations in the central and south-west Pacific, while British forces focused on Southeast Asia. This division allowed for coordinated offensives that overwhelmed Japanese defenses on multiple fronts. Meeting frequently throughout the war and in close contact with political leaders, the CCS ensured unity of command and purpose. Its coordinated approach was unprecedented and critical to the eventual Allied victory.","LocalizedString":"The Combined Chiefs of Staff (CCS) was formed in 1942 and became the central command body for Anglo-American military planning during WWII. Operating from Washington, it brought together senior US and British military leaders to harmonize strategy across all theaters. The CCS played a decisive role in shaping Allied operations, overseeing war production priorities, logistics, and theater command appointments. One of its earliest achievements was the \"Germany First\" strategy, which prioritized defeating Nazi Germany before focusing on Japan. The CCS also approved Operation Torch, the first major Allied amphibious assault in North Africa, and later coordinated the invasions of Sicily and Italy. Perhaps its most critical contribution came with Operation Overlord. The CCS selected General Eisenhower as Supreme Commander and directed the build-up for the 1944 Normandy landings. In the Pacific, the CCS established a division of responsibility: the US led operations in the central and south-west Pacific, while British forces focused on Southeast Asia. This division allowed for coordinated offensives that overwhelmed Japanese defenses on multiple fronts. Meeting frequently throughout the war and in close contact with political leaders, the CCS ensured unity of command and purpose. Its coordinated approach was unprecedented and critical to the eventual Allied victory."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'AU_AirCraft_Bomber_flyby_04'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Bomber/AU_AirCraft_Bomber_flyby_04.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/Homefront/T_combined_chief_of_staff.T_combined_chief_of_staff","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/COMBINED_CHIEFS_OF_STAFF.COMBINED_CHIEFS_OF_STAFF","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":411.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_combined_chiefs_of_staff_photo","SourceString":"Combined Chiefs of Staff meeting in Quebec, September 1944.","LocalizedString":"Com